{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's counterconditions leave a counterexample gap. The task domain can include deterministic, objective items that require expert inference not reducible to critical failure-mode checks; the checklist may yield 100% checklist compliance with zero errors on items it covers, while the panel still gets a critical branch wrong because the item required expert knowledge absent from the checklist. In such a single evaluation, there is no distinguishing evidence offered beyond the failure-mode framework, so genuine reliability gain cannot be separated from compliant box-checking that happens to be correct.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Tasks consist of objectively verifiable diagnostic outcomes where the critical decision hinges on expert-specific inference (e.g., interpreting a borderline diagnostic feature). The checklist enumerates failure modes but contains no item capturing the correct interpretation. Panel achieves 100% checklist compliance and answers wrong; expert intuition answers right."},{"distinguishing_evidence_within_single_evaluation":"SRC_001 provides no evidence type that separates procedural completeness from genuine reliability for items the checklist cannot encode."}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 explicitly detaches reliability from the checklist's content, but for objectively verifiable tasks the checklist encodes the decision rule linking observations to verifiable outcomes. A panel can have excellent social error-correction and full visible contestation, yet still answer incorrectly because the contested checklist item omits or misstates the objective criterion. The proposal offers no single-evaluation evidence that isolates the social correction effect from the checklist content; social visibility of disagreement does not distinguish whether the panel is reliably tracking the right answer or merely thoroughly executing the wrong checklist.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Panel debates a checklist item and reaches consensus via visible, contestable disagreement, but the checklist omits the objective criterion deciding the outcome. Social error-correction is high; accuracy is low."},{"single_evaluation_distinguishing_evidence":"No measure is proposed that separates genuine reliability gain from mere social compliance tracking the wrong checklist."}],"target_alias":"SRC_002"},{"attack":false,"case":"","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_003"}]}